<LabLayout title="Phobos experiment: model & assumptions · Tether Lab" description="The gravity, cable loads, release trajectories and energy accounting behind the Phobos Flight Studio." active="method">
  <main class="guide" id="lab-content">
    <header class="guide-header"><p class="micro">PHOBOS / P1-0.1.0</p><h1>A moon as the central anchor.</h1><p>Two radial cables turn with Phobos around Mars. Release from either end and see the trajectory, the cable loads and the cost of getting cargo there.</p><div class="guide-meta"><span>CIRCULAR BINARY</span><span>STATIC CABLE LOADS</span><span>ONE PAYLOAD RELEASE</span></div></header>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>What you are flying</h2><p>Phobos anchors an inward arm toward Mars and an outward arm away from it. This is a separate experiment from the freely rotating <a href="/lab/">Earth</a> and <a href="/lab/lunar/">Moon</a> facilities. It is inspired by the topology in <a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20030065879">Weinstein’s 2003 Phobos space-elevator proposal</a>; it does not reproduce that complete design.</p><p>The cargo begins at your chosen terminal, moving with it. Release adds no push. The calculation follows it under both Mars and Phobos gravity for two Phobos orbits, about 15.31 hours. The moon and its anchored arms continue along a prescribed circular orbit.</p><div class="scope-grid"><div class="scope-box"><h3>Calculated</h3><ul><li>Planar circular Mars–Phobos gravity</li><li>Inward or outward impulse-free cargo release</li><li>Uniform cable mass, terminal loads and tensile stress</li><li>Static loading before and after cargo leaves</li><li>An ideal surface-to-tip work and momentum budget</li></ul></div><div class="scope-box omitted"><h3>Outside this experiment</h3><ul><li>Incoming rendezvous, capture and cable interception</li><li>Climber motion, motor sizing and power efficiency</li><li>Flexible cables, anchor strength and Phobos terrain</li><li>Phobos’ response, recovery and repeated operation</li><li>Mars landing, solar targeting and ephemeris encounters</li></ul></div></div></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Gravity and clearance</h2><p>Frozen constants: Mars GM 4.282837362 × 10¹³ m³/s² and mean radius 3,389.5 km; Phobos GM 708,700 m³/s² and equivalent-volume radius 11.08 km; separation 9,375 km. These come from JPL’s <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/phys_par/">satellite physical parameters</a>, <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/phys_par.html">planetary parameters</a> and <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/elem/">mean orbital elements</a>, checked 19 September 2026. The orbit is circularized; these are not ephemerides.</p><p>In the rotating barycentric plane, Ω = μ<sub>M</sub>/r<sub>M</sub> + μ<sub>P</sub>/r<sub>P</sub> + n²(x² + y²)/2, with n² = (μ<sub>M</sub> + μ<sub>P</sub>)/a³. The motion follows x″ = Ω<sub>x</sub> + 2ny′ and y″ = Ω<sub>y</sub> − 2nx′. A fourth-order integrator advances at up to two seconds per step and checks the conserved Jacobi quantity, C = 2Ω − v².</p><p>The flight stops at the spherical Phobos surface or <strong>150 km above Mars</strong>. The Mars cutoff is a chosen exclusion boundary, not a physical atmosphere prediction. Periapsis and escape excess in the brief are instantaneous Mars-centered two-body values at release; the plotted path includes Phobos gravity. A clear run covers only the displayed duration.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Will the cable stay in tension?</h2><p>Each cable runs from Phobos’ spherical surface to a permanent 2 t terminal. That terminal stays after release. The calculation checks both arms unloaded and the selected arm with your payload. Along each radial arm, tension includes its distributed mass, Mars gravity, Phobos gravity and centrifugal acceleration.</p><p>For area A, density ρ and distance u from Phobos’ center, T(u) = m<sub>tip</sub>g(b) + ρA[Ω(b) − Ω(u)], where g is effective acceleration away from the anchor and b is the end distance. The interior tension maximum near the relevant libration point and both endpoints are explicitly checked. A cable requiring compression, exceeding its allowable stress or crossing the Mars exclusion boundary blocks launch.</p><p>The uniform Zylon HM and Kevlar 49 profiles use the lab’s existing <a href="/lab/method/#materials">sourced fiber values</a>, divided by your safety factor. These are not flight-qualified cables. Static checks do not establish survival of release waves, fatigue, surface anchoring or long-term operation.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="energy"><h2>Orbital energy is part of the bill.</h2><p>Positioning a payload along a corotating tether changes its angular momentum. P1 shows an ideal, quasistatic budget from the selected surface anchor to the tip, separate from the released flight.</p><div class="equation-panel"><p>ΔL = m n (x<sub>tip</sub>² − x<sub>surface</sub>²)</p><p>W<sub>anchor</sub> = n ΔL</p><p>W<sub>winch</sub> = −m (Ω<sub>tip</sub> − Ω<sub>surface</sub>)</p><p>ΔE<sub>payload</sub> = W<sub>anchor</sub> + W<sub>winch</sub></p></div><p>Coordinates and energy are barycentric. Positive anchor work is supplied from the prescribed binary’s orbital reservoir; negative work returns energy. A negative winch total indicates ideal net regeneration. It does not predict usable electricity, the local potential barrier near Phobos, peak power or motor losses.</p><p><strong>The anchor orbit is held fixed.</strong> Its actual response and replenishment are not simulated. A successful release therefore does not establish a repeatable transport service or free energy.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Try the two directions</h2><p>The default 1,250 km inward arm releases 3 t with about 502 km Mars periapsis. The 3,000 km outward arm gives about 1,029 m/s Mars escape excess at release, without targeting an interplanetary destination. The low-pass challenge starts with a 1,500 km inward arm: shorten it until the periapsis is 150–750 km, both cables carry their loads and the full flight clears the boundaries.</p><p>Phobos design storage, JSON exports and shared links are separate from Earth, Moon and Expeditions. Visiting this experiment does not change your campaign resources or progress. Continue your existing world when you return.</p><a class="button-link primary" href="/lab/phobos/">Open the Phobos experiment →</a></section>
  </main>
